# 46. Agent Role Taxonomy and Authority Boundaries

Every Agent must have one verified primary role and may have documented
secondary roles.

Role classification must follow real implementation and product purpose, not
the Agent name alone.

## 46.1 Evidence and Market-Analysis Agents

Typical current candidates:

- technical
- trend
- sentiment
- pattern
- fundamental
- market_intelligence
- volume

These Agents produce specialized evidence.

They may report:

- Direction
- Regime
- Strength
- Pattern
- Momentum
- Volume context
- Sentiment
- Fundamental context
- Market-event context
- Supporting evidence
- Conflicting evidence
- Freshness
- Data quality
- Limitations

They must not:

- Place orders
- Size positions
- Override Risk
- Claim execution approval
- Convert analysis into an irreversible side effect

## 46.2 Opportunity, Prediction and Timing Agents

Typical current candidates:

- price_prediction
- timing
- arbitrage

These Agents may produce:

- Forecast scenarios
- Opportunity candidates
- Timing windows
- Analytical spread opportunities
- Probability or confidence only when calibrated
- Invalidating conditions
- Time horizon
- Expiry

They must not:

- Present uncertainty as certainty
- Create execution instructions without downstream validation
- Trigger orders directly
- Bypass Risk, Portfolio, Liquidity or runtime policy

## 46.3 Control, Allocation and Optimization Agents

Typical current candidates:

- risk
- portfolio
- optimization

Risk Management has veto and limit authority when its canonical policy
conditions are satisfied.

Portfolio Allocation and Optimization may determine or recommend:

- Exposure
- Allocation
- Position-size bounds
- Diversification constraints
- Capital utilization
- Portfolio-level conflicts

They must not:

- Invent available balance
- Bypass canonical account/portfolio state
- Place orders directly
- Override Emergency Stop or runtime policy

## 46.4 Feasibility Agent

The current liquidity Agent is a likely execution-feasibility authority.

It may evaluate:

- Market depth
- Spread
- Slippage
- Tradable size
- Market impact
- Data freshness
- Execution feasibility

It must not determine market direction solely because an order book exists.

It must not place an order.

## 46.5 Execution Agent

The current order Agent is the execution component.

Order Management must:

- Receive an approved, bounded and traceable execution intent
- Revalidate all required gates
- Enforce idempotency
- Enforce confirmation where required
- Persist audit and provider response
- Reconcile the operation

Order Management must not:

- Decide market direction
- Manufacture profitability
- Replace Artemis
- Replace Risk
- Replace Portfolio sizing
- Replace Liquidity feasibility
- Execute from an untraceable Agent output

## 46.6 Role Changes

A role change is an architectural change.

Before changing an Agent role:

- Produce an RCA
- Identify old and new authority
- Identify affected consumers
- Identify safety impact
- Update contracts and tests
- Obtain explicit approval when authority increases

No Agent may silently gain veto, sizing, feasibility or execution authority.

